[[:db/retract 17592186159800 :answer-field/choices 17592186159801] [:db/retract 17592186159800 :answer-field/choices 17592186159802] [:db/retract 17592186159800 :answer-field/choices 17592186159803] [:db/retract 17592186159800 :answer-field/choices 17592186159804] [:db/retract 17592186159800 :answer-field/choices 17592186159805] [:db/retract 17592186159788 :answer-field/choices 17592186159792] [:db/retract 17592186159788 :answer-field/choices 17592186159793] [:db/retract 17592186159788 :answer-field/choices 17592186159789] [:db/retract 17592186159788 :answer-field/choices 17592186159790] [:db/retract 17592186159788 :answer-field/choices 17592186159791] {:db/id 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6a0ee3fc-f29f-5b1c-ac8e-c62ce346c425" "question-b7cf9f35-9682-5170-9481-47202753d57f" "question-d151529b-f776-5a6f-8050-189f72b4d535" "question-669ea571-4e73-5129-bf0b-c10dd97639ff"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161705 :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n = 360$ from a population where $35 \\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable $X_i$ be equal to\n![](images/9b/9b117bc5c640d338895493fde26f3c283d7752e1fcb7b0777a3e89a351808f6b.svg)\nwhere $i = 1, \\ldots, 360.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle X = \\sum_{i=1}^{360} X_i\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n![](images/ae/aee44a20433fe178eb22b1af7cf4d7e643b12bf4db69bd4b8a132cf8a59c50b3.svg)\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n,$ the random variables $X_i$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\nX_i\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p) \\qquad 1\\leq i\\leq n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X\\approx B(n,p),$ where\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np = 35 \\% = 0.35, \\qquad n = 360.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX\\approx B(360,0.35)\n$$\n\nand we have the following results for the mean and variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =np \\\\  & =360\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =126 \\\\ \\text{Var}[X] & =np(1-p) \\\\  & =360\\cdot 0.35\\cdot (1-0.35) \\\\  & =81.9\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1d5c4439-72cc-5a29-8805-27f9db8f5589" "question-548372c4-2d00-514c-b6a7-43bd98885c5f" "question-3d736eeb-4230-5251-a2a5-38a3eb699594" "question-2e62b0fb-05ae-5348-8fa8-26e1b9d8b974"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161703 :question/problem "Suppose that in a population of children with only one pet, $42\\%$ of children have a dog. A random sample of $n = 40$ children is taken from the population and asked what pet they have.\n\nWhat is the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p},$ the proportion of children in the sample that have a dog? Round your answers to $4$ decimal places where appropriate.\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables $X_i$ as follows:\n![](images/41/412ed703d6c44b1e664317fd734871cfe71d14c944cfa9f9bca84752f635836e.svg)\n\nNow, provided that $N \\gg n,$ we have that\n\n$$\nX = \\sum_{i=1}^n X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that have the characteristic, and \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p).\n$$\n\nThe sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p}  = \\overline{X} = \\dfrac{X}{n}.\n$$\n\nUsing the properties of expectation and variance, it is straightforward to show that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p} ] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $p = 42 \\% = 0.42,$ and $n = 40.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =42%=0.42, \\\\ \\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{\\,p}] & =\\frac{0.42(1-0.42)}{40} \\\\  & ≈0.0061\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162138 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0a412ed0-e9f7-5318-a109-33611797297f" "question-dcf2c7ff-5863-50a2-8613-6f8998a6f4cd" "question-f68dd177-6fb9-5034-8aaa-03b4d052bc7a" "question-c6b56e16-5d64-5848-860d-3d44e0d9f335"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-0a412ed0-e9f7-5318-a109-33611797297f" :question/id #uuid "0a412ed0-e9f7-5318-a109-33611797297f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146348" :question/problem "Suppose we have a population of size $N=75,$ where $80%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,75.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0).$" :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N=75,$ and $p=0.8$ represents the proportion of the population with the characteristic. \n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of population members *with* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nNp=75(0.8)=60,\n$$\n\n- the number of population members *without* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nN(1-p)=75(1-0.8)=15.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second member does not have the characteristic given that the first member does not have the characteristic either.\n\nIf the first member does not have the characteristic, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=75-1=74$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $N(1-p)-1=14$ members remaining who do not have the characteristic.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{N(1-p)-1}{N-1}=\\frac{14}{74}=\\frac{7}{37}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aefeda84-cb3e-54c7-a117-3708338c33ac"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aefeda84-cb3e-54c7-a117-3708338c33ac" :answer-field/id #uuid "aefeda84-cb3e-54c7-a117-3708338c33ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8503818a-9b86-5a35-aecb-0a2e8b2fe3bb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-98d6ca49-3887-5fb0-bc6d-0177b078f0c2" "answer-7dd97489-93fd-5274-88c5-b2b19f1359a8" "answer-6a104a41-6eed-5c24-af7a-71a6fe434f4f" "answer-068df0c4-ece2-551e-830d-a33b8a7d322a" "answer-8503818a-9b86-5a35-aecb-0a2e8b2fe3bb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98d6ca49-3887-5fb0-bc6d-0177b078f0c2" :answer/id #uuid "98d6ca49-3887-5fb0-bc6d-0177b078f0c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{37}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dd97489-93fd-5274-88c5-b2b19f1359a8" :answer/id #uuid "7dd97489-93fd-5274-88c5-b2b19f1359a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15}{74}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a104a41-6eed-5c24-af7a-71a6fe434f4f" :answer/id #uuid "6a104a41-6eed-5c24-af7a-71a6fe434f4f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-068df0c4-ece2-551e-830d-a33b8a7d322a" :answer/id #uuid "068df0c4-ece2-551e-830d-a33b8a7d322a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{59}{74}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8503818a-9b86-5a35-aecb-0a2e8b2fe3bb" :answer/id #uuid "8503818a-9b86-5a35-aecb-0a2e8b2fe3bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{37}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162425 :question/problem "Suppose we have a population of size $N=20,$ where $55%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,20.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1).$" :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N=20,$ and $p=0.55$ represents the proportion of the population with the characteristic. \n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of population members *with* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nNp=20(0.55)=11,\n$$\n\n- the number of population members *without* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nN(1-p)=20(1-0.55)=9.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1),$ the probability that the second member has the characteristic given that the first member also has the characteristic.\n\nIf the first member has the characteristic, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=20-1=19$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np-1=11-1=10$ members remaining that have the characteristic.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1)=\\frac{Np-1}{N-1}=\\frac{10}{19}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159752 :answer-field/correct 17592186159753 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-dcf2c7ff-5863-50a2-8613-6f8998a6f4cd" :question/id #uuid "dcf2c7ff-5863-50a2-8613-6f8998a6f4cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-146350" :question/problem "Suppose we have a population of size $N=180,$ where $45%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,180.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1).$" :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N=180,$ and $p=0.45$ represents the proportion of the population with the characteristic. \n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of population members *with* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nNp=180(0.45)=81,\n$$\n\n- the number of population members *without* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nN(1-p)=180(1-0.45)=99.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1),$ the probability that the second member has the characteristic given that the first member also has the characteristic.\n\nIf the first member has the characteristic, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=180-1=179$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np-1=81-1=80$ members remaining that have the characteristic.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1)=\\frac{Np-1}{N-1}=\\frac{80}{179}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-89a7acda-4b0c-5959-9813-189a818f04e8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-89a7acda-4b0c-5959-9813-189a818f04e8" :answer-field/id #uuid "89a7acda-4b0c-5959-9813-189a818f04e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1d71ebcd-0854-5420-800e-e81f47fa3a93" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7643c0f8-c498-5e7d-8da6-ee8fc999539f" "answer-1d71ebcd-0854-5420-800e-e81f47fa3a93" "answer-fd4eb93f-8444-5e43-b500-251824ded3fd" "answer-df392b8d-2114-58ba-a19c-702b03b86bc5" "answer-0fd116ef-3346-54bc-b2fe-60c06789bd85"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7643c0f8-c498-5e7d-8da6-ee8fc999539f" :answer/id #uuid "7643c0f8-c498-5e7d-8da6-ee8fc999539f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{11}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d71ebcd-0854-5420-800e-e81f47fa3a93" :answer/id #uuid "1d71ebcd-0854-5420-800e-e81f47fa3a93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{80}{179}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd4eb93f-8444-5e43-b500-251824ded3fd" :answer/id #uuid "fd4eb93f-8444-5e43-b500-251824ded3fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{98}{179}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df392b8d-2114-58ba-a19c-702b03b86bc5" :answer/id #uuid "df392b8d-2114-58ba-a19c-702b03b86bc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{99}{179}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fd116ef-3346-54bc-b2fe-60c06789bd85" :answer/id #uuid "0fd116ef-3346-54bc-b2fe-60c06789bd85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f68dd177-6fb9-5034-8aaa-03b4d052bc7a" :question/id #uuid "f68dd177-6fb9-5034-8aaa-03b4d052bc7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-146378" :question/problem "A children's soccer academy has $60$ children enrolled. From this population, $35%$ are left-footed. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population is left-footed} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,60.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0).$" :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N=60,$ and $p=0.35$ represents the proportion of the population of left-footed children.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of  left-footed children equals\n\n$$\nNp=60(0.35)=21,\n$$\n\n- the number of right-footed children equals\n\n$$\nN(1-p)=60(1-0.35)=39.\n$$\n\nWe select two children of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second child is left-footed given that the first child is right-footed.\n\nIf the first child is right-footed, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=60-1=59$ children remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np=21$ children remaining who are left-footed.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{Np}{N-1}=\\frac{21}{59}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c3b51b3f-c022-5825-8c4e-e77f4df70c5f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c3b51b3f-c022-5825-8c4e-e77f4df70c5f" :answer-field/id #uuid "c3b51b3f-c022-5825-8c4e-e77f4df70c5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f913f1e1-b98c-5a22-87cd-ede32839a4d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-530a30e5-6563-5fe0-b636-336bb2bc199c" "answer-82ad77ef-31e8-5b07-b94f-c8afee64c5ce" "answer-f913f1e1-b98c-5a22-87cd-ede32839a4d6" "answer-a9e62035-ace1-5772-89b1-118dc2b95cbb" "answer-2a5e0608-9752-57c8-81ea-c146ec7ac93b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-530a30e5-6563-5fe0-b636-336bb2bc199c" :answer/id #uuid "530a30e5-6563-5fe0-b636-336bb2bc199c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{13}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82ad77ef-31e8-5b07-b94f-c8afee64c5ce" :answer/id #uuid "82ad77ef-31e8-5b07-b94f-c8afee64c5ce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{20}{59}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f913f1e1-b98c-5a22-87cd-ede32839a4d6" :answer/id #uuid "f913f1e1-b98c-5a22-87cd-ede32839a4d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{21}{59}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a9e62035-ace1-5772-89b1-118dc2b95cbb" :answer/id #uuid "a9e62035-ace1-5772-89b1-118dc2b95cbb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{38}{59}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a5e0608-9752-57c8-81ea-c146ec7ac93b" :answer/id #uuid "2a5e0608-9752-57c8-81ea-c146ec7ac93b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{20}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c6b56e16-5d64-5848-860d-3d44e0d9f335" :question/id #uuid "c6b56e16-5d64-5848-860d-3d44e0d9f335" :question/math-academy-id "q-146352" :question/problem "A box contains $70$ marbles, of which $30%$ are red and the rest are green. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th marble in the box is red} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,70.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0).$" :question/worked-solution "We have a population of size $N=70,$ and $p=0.3$ represents the proportion of the red marbles.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of red marbles equals \n\n$$\nNp=70(0.3)=21,\n$$\n\n- the number of green marbles equals\n\n$$\nN(1-p)=70(1-0.3)=49.\n$$\n\nWe select two marbles at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second marble is green given that the first marble is also green.\n\nIf the first marble is green, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=70-1=69$ marbles remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $N(1-p)-1=48$ marbles remaining that are green.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{N(1-p)-1}{N-1}=\\frac{48}{69}=\\frac{16}{23}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e12b02a5-0ff9-56c2-b879-a7b367d95c2e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e12b02a5-0ff9-56c2-b879-a7b367d95c2e" :answer-field/id #uuid "e12b02a5-0ff9-56c2-b879-a7b367d95c2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f47bceb2-4874-5deb-9f1d-7a538bff5d23" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f47bceb2-4874-5deb-9f1d-7a538bff5d23" "answer-094ec17a-6309-5f98-acf9-535d8eb17412" "answer-62080c1c-fa0a-59e1-92cf-4e1e24714793" "answer-bf63837b-36e5-5303-a79a-c2032f377b9b" "answer-fa6e404a-f691-5fa0-8933-b5686b52a70e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f47bceb2-4874-5deb-9f1d-7a538bff5d23" :answer/id #uuid "f47bceb2-4874-5deb-9f1d-7a538bff5d23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{16}{23}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-094ec17a-6309-5f98-acf9-535d8eb17412" :answer/id #uuid "094ec17a-6309-5f98-acf9-535d8eb17412" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{17}{23}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62080c1c-fa0a-59e1-92cf-4e1e24714793" :answer/id #uuid "62080c1c-fa0a-59e1-92cf-4e1e24714793" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{20}{69}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf63837b-36e5-5303-a79a-c2032f377b9b" :answer/id #uuid "bf63837b-36e5-5303-a79a-c2032f377b9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{49}{69}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fa6e404a-f691-5fa0-8933-b5686b52a70e" :answer/id #uuid "fa6e404a-f691-5fa0-8933-b5686b52a70e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9}{23}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6a0ee3fc-f29f-5b1c-ac8e-c62ce346c425" :question/id #uuid "6a0ee3fc-f29f-5b1c-ac8e-c62ce346c425" :question/math-academy-id "q-146399" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=130$ from a population where $35%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,130.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{130}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=35%=0.35,\\,n=130.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(130,0.35).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eb58af87-7a8d-591a-a5c6-db3582a4b58b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eb58af87-7a8d-591a-a5c6-db3582a4b58b" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb58af87-7a8d-591a-a5c6-db3582a4b58b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-313d69b4-6852-5a13-b565-58db94680187" :answer-field/choices ["answer-313d69b4-6852-5a13-b565-58db94680187" "answer-3c5516fc-3456-5737-8aa9-330d31b2be53" "answer-754cb5ea-c2f6-5ee8-b725-93337b939d1b" "answer-9c96fe6b-2a87-55d0-9ea0-ffcdcef3271e" "answer-d1480c9b-f23c-52a6-b9ab-57d01ff0f089"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-313d69b4-6852-5a13-b565-58db94680187" :answer/id #uuid "313d69b4-6852-5a13-b565-58db94680187" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B(130,0.35)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c5516fc-3456-5737-8aa9-330d31b2be53" :answer/id #uuid "3c5516fc-3456-5737-8aa9-330d31b2be53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B(130,0.65)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-754cb5ea-c2f6-5ee8-b725-93337b939d1b" :answer/id #uuid "754cb5ea-c2f6-5ee8-b725-93337b939d1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B(46,0.35)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c96fe6b-2a87-55d0-9ea0-ffcdcef3271e" :answer/id #uuid "9c96fe6b-2a87-55d0-9ea0-ffcdcef3271e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.35)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1480c9b-f23c-52a6-b9ab-57d01ff0f089" :answer/id #uuid "d1480c9b-f23c-52a6-b9ab-57d01ff0f089" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Bernoulli}(0.65)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b7cf9f35-9682-5170-9481-47202753d57f" :question/id #uuid "b7cf9f35-9682-5170-9481-47202753d57f" :question/math-academy-id "q-146402" :question/problem "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=180$ from a population where $48%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,180.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{180}{X}_{i}.$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a population of size $N,$ and we define the sequence of random variables ${X}_{i}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, provided that $N≫n,$ the random variables ${X}_{i}$ can be considered independent and identically distributed. Under this assumption, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(p)\\,1\\le i\\le n\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the proportion of the population that has the characteristic.\n\nTherefore, the random variable $X,$ defined as \n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated as a binomial random variable $X≈B(n,p).$ \n\nIn our case, we're told that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size. Moreover, we have \n\n$$\np=48%=0.48,\\,n=180.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nX≈B(180,0.48).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d7223c07-8a22-51f9-b040-0c281e3688fe"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d7223c07-8a22-51f9-b040-0c281e3688fe" :answer-field/id #uuid "d7223c07-8a22-51f9-b040-0c281e3688fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3dc1bec2-caea-5850-8341-0f2e65eb3ee9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3dc1bec2-caea-5850-8341-0f2e65eb3ee9" 